t, node1 never became reachable again despite dozens of polling attempts across the captured timeline (cmd-4facb5a928d3400d through cmd-141b87e9b5bf416d, and the executor's own report of continued polling beyond 90 minutes), and the after_executor lifecycle snapshot independently confirms total unreachability (all observations returned Connection reset by peer). This leaves the node, and therefore the final verifiable state of the /srv/data deliverable, unconfirmed and the managed node effectively down at the end of the run. Causation cannot be established with certainty from the captured evidence (it could be an infrastructure/hypervisor issue rather than the custom unit), but the risky, non-default boot-time dependency the executor introduced is a plausible contributing factor and materially degrades the safety/quality of the final state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-03080de8b59548b6\",\"cmd-63e55450f3744743\",\"cmd-4facb5a928d3400d\",\"cmd-141b87e9b5bf416d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the public task contract -- an LVM logical volume mounted at /srv/data, and growing it online to at least 2 GB (2.5 GiB) with contents verified intact before and after -- are directly and coherently satisfied by evidence captured prior to the mandated post-task reboot. The subsequent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol, however, was not completed: node1 never came back online after systemctl reboot was issued, so no post-restart boot identity or service/data recovery could be observed. Because persistence-across-reboot was not part of the task's own contract, this does not reduce the task-functionality verdict, but it does leave the final health of node1 and its long-term durability entirely unverified.\",\"limitations\":[\"No post-restart evidence exists for node1: boot identity, mount/LV state, and data integrity after the systemctl reboot could not be observed because the node became permanently unreachable (Co